     for right, x in enumerate(nums):\n            if x == 0:\n                zeros += 1\n            while zeros > k:\n                if nums[left] == 0:\n                    zeros -= 1\n                left += 1\n            best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n        return best\n","approach":"**Longest window with at most k zeros.** Flipping up to `k` zeros inside a window makes it all ones, so the question is purely about zero counts. Expand `right`, counting zeros; while there are more than `k`, advance `left`, decrementing the count when a zero leaves. The window is always valid after the shrink step, so its length is a candidate answer.\n\n**Why it works:** for each `right`, the loop leaves `left` at the smallest index such that `nums[left..right]` has at most `k` zeros, so every maximal valid window is measured. `left` only moves right, giving O(n).\n\n**Common mistakes:** actually mutating the array to flip zeros (unnecessary and makes the shrink step wrong); forgetting that `k = 0` must still work (longest run of existing ones); mixing up the roles of the counters.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Delete exactly one element and maximise the run of ones (window with at most one zero, answer is length minus one). Return the indices to flip (the zeros in the best window). Generalise to 'at most k elements not equal to v' for any value v, which is the character replacement problem.","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["longest-repeating-character-replacement","fruit-into-baskets","longest-subarray-of-1s-after-deleting-one-element"],"number":27,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def longestOnes(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n        pass\n"}